+# SPEC: Renewals
+
+## Why
+
+Members ask to keep books longer. Today staff do that by returning the copy and
+checking it out again, which splits one borrowing into two loans and breaks the
+loan history. A renewal extends the open loan in place: same loan, new due date.
+Renewing must not become a way around the rules: lateness built up before a
+renewal is still charged, a member can't keep a copy others are queuing for, and
+only members who are paid up can renew.
+
+(The previous spec, holds, is in git history. Its rules live on in
+`docs/adr/0003`, `docs/adr/0005` and `GLOSSARY.md`. The renewal rules settled
+earlier are in `docs/adr/0004` and `docs/adr/0006`, and the ones settled for this
+work are in `docs/adr/0007`.)
+
+## Interface (fixed; other code calls exactly this)
+
+- `LendingService.renew(loan_id: str) -> Loan` in `src/lending/service.py`: renews
+  the open loan in place (same `loan_id`) and returns it.
+- `Loan` in `src/lending/models.py` gets a field `renewals: int` (default `0`): how
+  many times the loan has been renewed.
+- Add `RenewalRefused(LendingError)` to `src/lending/errors.py` for renewals that
+  can't be granted. Unknown loan ids keep raising `LoanNotFound`, and the other
+  existing error classes are kept where they already fit.
+
+## Rules
+
+- **What can be renewed.** Only an open loan of a **book**. DVDs are never
+  renewable.
+- **Renewal limit.** A loan can be renewed at most **2** times.
+- **Renewal window.** A loan can be renewed from **5 days before its current due
+  date** onwards. There is no upper limit, so an overdue loan can be renewed.
+- **New due date.** It is counted from the renewal day, exactly as for a checkout
+  made that day: + 21 days, with Sunday → Monday.
+- **Queue block** (ADR 0006). There is no renewal while the title has any WAITING
+  hold. READY holds don't block.
+- **Paid up** (ADR 0006). The member's balance must be exactly 0.00 before the
+  renewal. This applies to adults and children alike.
+- **Accrued fine** (ADR 0004). Renewing an overdue loan charges the fine built up
+  so far, as if the copy had been returned on the renewal day: the usual late
+  days, rates and child exemption. Lateness after that counts from the new due
+  date.
+- **Fine cap per loan** (ADR 0007). The replacement-cost cap applies to the total
+  overdue fines charged for one loan, across its renewals and the return
+  together.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+The examples use the test clock, which starts on Monday 2 March 2026. Book copy B1
+(title "978-1", replacement cost 18.00, 0.25 a late day) is borrowed by adult Ann
+that day and is due Monday 23 March.
+
+Granting a renewal
+
+1. `renew` returns the same loan (same `loan_id`, `copy_id`, `member_id` and
+   `borrowed_on`). It is still open, its `due_on` is the new due date and its
+   `renewals` has gone up by 1. `get_loan(loan_id)` shows the same values.
+2. A new loan has `renewals == 0`.
+3. The new due date is the renewal day + 21 days (Sunday → Monday). Renewed Thu 19
+   March → due Thu 9 April. Renewed Sun 22 March → Sun 12 April, which moves to
+   Mon 13 April.
+4. After a renewal the copy stays `ON_LOAN`, and `loan_history` / `open_loans` show
+   one loan, not two.
+5. Renewing an on-time loan charges nothing.
+
+Window and limit
+
+6. Due Mon 23 March: renewing on Tue 17 March raises `RenewalRefused`, and on Wed
+   18 March it succeeds. Renewing on the due date, or any day after it, also
+   succeeds.
+7. The window always counts from the loan's *current* due date. Straight after a
+   renewal, renewing again on the same day is refused.
+8. The first and second renewals succeed, and the third raises `RenewalRefused`
+   even inside the window. `renewals` stays 2.
+
+Refusals
+
+9. An unknown `loan_id` raises `LoanNotFound`.
+10. A loan that has been returned raises `RenewalRefused`.
+11. A loan of a DVD raises `RenewalRefused`, even inside the window.
+12. A WAITING hold on the loan's title raises `RenewalRefused`. Once the title has
+    only READY holds (or none), the loan can be renewed.
+13. Any balance above 0.00 (even 0.01, from a fine, manual charge or no-show fee)
+    raises `AccountBlocked`, for adults and children alike. After it is paid off,
+    the renewal succeeds.
+14. A refused renewal changes nothing: `due_on`, `renewals`, the balance and the
+    copy's status are as they were.
+
+Lateness is never lost
+
+15. Renewing an overdue loan charges the fine built up so far to the member's
+    balance. Renewed Mon 30 March (6 late days) → 1.50 is charged, and the loan is
+    then due Mon 20 April.
+16. That fine doesn't block the renewal that charges it, but it does block the next
+    renewal (`AccountBlocked`) until it is paid.
+17. After an overdue renewal, the return counts lateness only from the new due date.
+    In the example above, returning on Wed 22 April adds 0.50, so the loan's fines
+    total 2.00.
+18. A child renewing an overdue loan is charged nothing, and the renewal is granted.
+19. The total of all overdue fines for one loan never exceeds the title's
+    replacement cost. For a book costing 2.00, renewing 4 late days after the due
+    date charges 1.00. Returning 6 late days after the new due date then charges
+    only the remaining 1.00 (not 1.50), so the loan's fines total 2.00.
+
+General
+
+20. `overdue_loans` and the overdue report use the renewed due date.
+21. All existing tests still pass.
+
+## Non-goals
+
+- Renewing DVDs, or a different renewal limit or window per material or per
+  member category.
+- A log of each renewal (dates, old due dates). The `renewals` count is the only
+  record, besides the fine entries that overdue renewals charge.
+- Renewing several loans at once, or renewing by copy barcode or by member.
+- Notifying members about due dates or renewals.
+- Which error is raised when several refusals apply to one `renew` call.
+- Changing how fines are charged on return, apart from the per-loan cap.
