+# 0001 — Overdue fines
+
+## Context
+
+Late returns cost nothing, so DVDs come back weeks late. The owner set the fine
+rules on 2026-10-28.
+
+## Decision
+
+- A fine is charged **when the copy is returned**, never while the loan is still
+  out. Each late return adds its own fine to the member's account. (The one
+  exception is renewing an overdue loan, which charges the fine so far: ADR 0004.)
+- **Late days** are the open days after the due date, up to and including the
+  return day. Sundays (closed) don't count, because nobody could return anything
+  then. There is no grace period. This is deliberately not the overdue report's
+  calendar-day count.
+- Rate per late day depends on material: book 0.25, DVD 1.00
+  (`policy.FINE_PER_DAY`).
+- One loan's fine is capped at the title's replacement cost. Since renewals, this
+  is the total over the loan's renewals and return (ADR 0007).
+- Children (`MemberCategory.CHILD`) are never charged overdue fines.
+- The calculation lives in one place, `policy.late_fine(...)`, and takes the
+  "as of" day as a parameter. Every later feature that charges lateness
+  (renewals, see ADR 0004) must use it rather than re-derive the rules.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- A member who never returns a copy never builds up a fine on their balance.
+  Only the loan limit and the overdue report hold them back.
+- Two numbers called "days late" exist: the report's calendar days and the
+  fine's late days. The glossary keeps the two apart.
+- Changing the rates later would alter only future charges. Past fines are
+  stored as amounts, not recomputed.
